-- title:
--   Theorem 2.5: Structure theorem for admissible Lie algebras
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Every admissible $\mathfrak g$ admits a structural Spindler presentation $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak z\oplus V\oplus\mathfrak l$, with the bracket of equation (5), such that: (a) $\mathfrak z=\mathfrak z(\mathfrak g)$ and $\mathfrak u=\mathfrak z\oplus V$ is the largest nilpotent ideal; (b) $\mathfrak l$ is reductive and its simple ideals are compact or hermitian; (c) $\mathfrak t_l$ is a compactly embedded Cartan subalgebra of $\mathfrak l$ and $\mathfrak t=\mathfrak z\oplus\mathfrak t_l$ is one of $\mathfrak g$; (d) $V=[\mathfrak t,\mathfrak u]=[\mathfrak t_l,V]$; and (e) there are $f\in\mathfrak z^*$ and $y\in\mathfrak l$ such that $f\circ\beta$ is symplectic and
--
--   $$f(\beta(y.v,v))>0\quad(0\ne v\in V).$$
--
--   Conversely, a structural Spindler presentation satisfying (a)–(e) is admissible. All presentation data in the forward direction are existential conclusions. This is the structural interface for the later results.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 4, Theorem 2.5: Structure theorem for admissible Lie algebras

